WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.434
-Thank you for coming on.
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:05.033
What a thrill.
-You look great. Dude --

00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:06.734
-Thank you.
Lilac for you tonight, Jimmy.

00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:08.567
-You really did lilac for me.
And I love it.

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:10.501
Thank you so much.
But I also --

00:00:10.501 --> 00:00:12.300
Dude, I'm loving the beard.
Are you gonna shave that off?

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.133
-I am -- At the moment I step
out of that theater in January.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:17.734
I'm going to --
I'm going to trim it.

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:20.467
I'm not going to shave it all
because it's contour for men.

00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:22.968
It gives me a nice, like --
No, you look great.

00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:25.968
-I do like it. I wasn't able to
grow one for a really long time.

00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:27.901
So now that I have one,

00:00:27.901 --> 00:00:29.200
I might keep it
for a little while.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.701
-I think you should.
It looks really good.

00:00:30.701 --> 00:00:31.934
I just --
Sometimes you feel like

00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:33.567
when you're forced
to have a beard

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:35.400
like you have to have
in "Sweeney Todd,"

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.801
you're like --
As soon as the play is done,

00:00:36.801 --> 00:00:38.100
you're like,
"That's it. I'm shaving it."

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.567
-I actually kind of
had to pitch it

00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:41.400
because, like,
a barber with a beard,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.534
like, you're not selling much
if you're -- if you're --

00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:46.100
-I guess. Yeah.
A barber with a beard.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.000
No, but he wouldn't --
-Thought it looked cool.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.033
Like, "Yeah, you can do that.
Sure."

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:52.334
-"Yeah." I want to show
a picture of you.

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:54.167
This was --
-Who's that baby?

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:56.567
-Yeah. Look at this baby here.
This is "Closer."

00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:59.601
-Who's that kid? [ Laughs ]
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:03.334
-This is the 20th anniversary?
-20th anniversary of that album.

00:01:03.334 --> 00:01:05.067
Yeah, it was an album
that really changed my life.

00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:07.334
And I can't believe the time
has flown. The song --

00:01:07.334 --> 00:01:08.968
-20 years. Josh, that's crazy.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:12.534
-I'm an old.
-No, you're not an old.

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:14.834
I'm an old.
But, I mean, this is wild.

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:16.367
November 17th, this comes out.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.367
-This comes out.
And so many of the songs

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:19.734
on this album changed my life

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:21.400
and have been a part of
so many people's lives.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.267
And when they write me letters,
they talk about a lot

00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:25.367
of the songs on this album.
So we're putting it out

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:27.033
with some extra songs
and bonus stuff,

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:29.234
some studio stuff
that's not been released before.

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:31.701
-This was -- "You Raise Me Up"
was on this album?

00:01:31.701 --> 00:01:34.400
-It was. Yeah. And --
-That was the biggest song.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.667
-Oh, my.
Every wedding, every funeral.

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:40.067
-Yeah. Both. Yeah.
You had them both covered.

00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:42.133
You had them both covered.
-And everything in between.

00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:43.868
-No, but I remember
when that song came out,

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:46.667
it was like --
I saw it in places. I go --

00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:48.467
Everyone loved it.
It was on commercials.

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:50.067
It was on everything. I go --

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:52.267
It was part of the, like --
I want to say the Olympics.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.334
Was it a meaningful moment?
-It was omnipresent.

00:01:54.334 --> 00:01:55.868
-Yes.
-It really was. Yeah.

00:01:55.868 --> 00:01:58.267
I mean, it's such a lucky thing
when you have a song like that,

00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:01.067
that has been everywhere,
and I remember the first time

00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:03.601
I heard it in the back of
a taxi cab in Dublin, Ireland,

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:05.167
when I was promoting
my first album.

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:06.434
And then at some point somebody

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:07.734
came into the studio
with it and was like,

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:09.167
"Hey, I think you'd be
really right for that song."

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:12.501
I was like, "That was my drunken
Irish taxi cab song."

00:02:12.501 --> 00:02:14.000
-Yeah.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.601
-And we never expected it to be
a single, and it just blew up.

00:02:16.601 --> 00:02:19.267
-I -- Congrats on that.
And, by the way, congrats

00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:21.434
on the 20th anniversary
deluxe edition.

00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:24.834
Pick this up. And it's got
some extra Easter eggs.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:27.167
Got some Easter eggs in there
for fans.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:28.701
-Yeah, it does. Definitely.
Yeah.

00:02:28.701 --> 00:02:30.300
There's a verse
that we had cut out

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.734
of "You Raise Me Up" early on,
and we put it back in.

00:02:33.734 --> 00:02:36.033
-Yes!
-Now it's 17 minutes longer.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:39.234
-Yeah. Oh, my god. Fantastic.
So it's a quadruple album.

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:42.467
-That's right. It is. Yeah.
-But I was talking to you.

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:44.934
I was lucky enough to go
see you at "Sweeney Todd,"

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:46.701
but we were talking backstage.

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:48.534
"Sweeney Todd."
You crush this.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.734
-Thank you.
-And you were saying to me

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:52.901
that you were kind of
a theater nerd.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.601
-You were a theater kid.
Yeah. Still am.

00:02:54.601 --> 00:02:57.667
Yeah, I --
You know, Sweeney Todd

00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:59.067
is one of those iconic roles

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:01.133
that for those of us
that grew up,

00:03:01.133 --> 00:03:02.901
you know,
with baritone voices

00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:06.667
and just, you know, not really
having the ability to dance,

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:08.701
there were certain roles
that were like,

00:03:08.701 --> 00:03:10.567
"Oh, I want to
dive into that one day."

00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:12.534
And "Sweeney Todd"
is just one of the most

00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:14.734
brilliant pieces of music
ever written.

00:03:14.734 --> 00:03:16.701
I did it
when I was in summer camp

00:03:16.701 --> 00:03:18.033
at Interlochen Arts Camp
in Michigan.

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.133
-No way.
-I was in the ensemble

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:20.434
and had the best time
of my life.

00:03:20.434 --> 00:03:22.968
-And here you are on Broadway.
It's so full circle.

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:24.868
-Getting nominated
for a Tony Award.

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:27.968
Not too shabby, buddy.
-It's been really amazing.

00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:29.667
Our cast and crew...
-Little Josh Groban.

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:31.133
If you're watching.
-Little Joshy.

00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:32.667
-Little Joshy,
if you're watching,

00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:33.767
you did pretty well, buddy.

00:03:33.767 --> 00:03:35.167
-In another dimension somewhere.

00:03:35.167 --> 00:03:37.033
-Yes. It might happen.
Who knows where this show airs.

00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:39.734
-Who knows?
-I got to see it opening night.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:43.100
-Yes, you did.
-Of course I was crying.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.567
I was laughing.
Because it's very dramatic.

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:47.701
-We loved hearing you laugh.
Can I tell you -- on the stage,

00:03:47.701 --> 00:03:50.834
we could hear you laughing
and we're like, "Jimmy's here!"

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:53.300
-Oh, did you hear it?
-Was I distracting?

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.267
-No, it wasn't distracting.
-It was so funny.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.000
-We felt --
we felt the love out there,

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.133
and it was so, so awesome.

00:03:58.133 --> 00:03:59.734
-Annaleigh Ashford
was unbelievable.

00:03:59.734 --> 00:04:01.934
-Annaleigh Ashford
is incredible. Gaten Matarazzo.

00:04:01.934 --> 00:04:03.200
I mean, this cast is so great,

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.234
and there is a lot
of dark humor in the show,

00:04:05.234 --> 00:04:06.868
which not a lot of people
sometimes know

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:09.167
when they --
when they get the premise of --

00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:11.300
-Oh, I was --
I thought it was well-directed.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.968
It was so funny. Every time --

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:15.601
I mean, obviously,
if you don't know the story,

00:04:15.601 --> 00:04:17.334
I'm gonna spoil it for you.

00:04:17.334 --> 00:04:20.100
But he's a barber
and he kills people.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.267
-He kills -- He dabbles.
He dabbles in killing.

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:25.200
-He dabbles in killing people,
and he uses the meat

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.033
they serve in meat pies
and sell it to everybody.

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.701
Anyway...
-This is after dinner, right?

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.567
-It's very -- Yes.
But when you --

00:04:31.567 --> 00:04:34.467
You take 'em upstairs
to this barber chair,

00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:37.701
and you slit their throats,
and then you pull the lever...

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:39.601
and the body goes out.

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:44.200
And I laughed every single time.
I go, "That's fantastic."

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.868
Except for --
Except for when the judge --

00:04:46.868 --> 00:04:47.934
-Spoiler alert.

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:49.300
-Again, spoiler alert.
Whatever.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.400
-But some people die,
and the chair is the coolest toy

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.834
because it is built --
It is a work of art.

00:04:54.834 --> 00:04:56.934
This chair belongs in a museum
one day.

00:04:56.934 --> 00:04:59.968
But it is also built like
an old antique, like, Winnebago.

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:01.067
Like, there's a clutch.

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:02.734
There's a lever.
It clanks. It's heavy.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:04.434
-Do you have to,
like, press the thing?

00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:06.868
-I have to press a thing and
pull a thing at the right time

00:05:06.868 --> 00:05:08.234
so the hook goes into here.

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:09.934
-Then the body slides
down a slide, off the stage.

00:05:09.934 --> 00:05:11.434
-Let me tell you --
they lube up the chair

00:05:11.434 --> 00:05:12.901
pretty good before the show.

00:05:12.901 --> 00:05:18.567
And so the actors just go way --
I mean, they are like, "Whee!"

00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:22.267
And -- Sometimes I hear
a "whee" on the way down. Yeah.

00:05:22.267 --> 00:05:25.234
And sometimes the chair --
Early on in previews,

00:05:25.234 --> 00:05:27.033
the chair kind of decided
it was gonna be like,

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:29.467
"Hm, not today, Sweeney.
I'm not gonna do this today."

00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:31.367
So the chair for a couple

00:05:31.367 --> 00:05:33.200
of the early performances,
stopped working.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.033
And that's, like,
my whole shtick in this show,

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:36.801
if you see it, like --

00:05:36.801 --> 00:05:38.801
I finally get that chair
and I get to use the lever

00:05:38.801 --> 00:05:40.200
and I get to do the thing.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.133
So when the chair stopped
working, all of a sudden,

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:44.701
like, we had dead bodies
that were like...

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:47.200
[ Laughter ]

00:05:49.968 --> 00:05:52.100
-Face-first down the slide.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.033
-Crawling down
their own death hole.

00:05:54.033 --> 00:05:56.801
-And then -- And then
they're also saying, "Whee."

00:05:56.801 --> 00:05:58.901
-Yeah. "Whee!"
-You don't have to say "whee."

00:05:58.901 --> 00:06:01.334
You're not going fast. Yeah.
That's hilarious.

00:06:01.334 --> 00:06:03.701
Did the audience go with it?
-Yeah. Of course.

00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:05.701
Because they knew
we were in previews,

00:06:05.701 --> 00:06:07.534
and that's when stuff
is getting changed anyway.

00:06:07.534 --> 00:06:09.400
But the audiences have been
right there with us.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.167
And they do -- They laugh
when I send dead bodies.

00:06:12.167 --> 00:06:14.801
Shame on you.
-No. No, no.

00:06:14.801 --> 00:06:16.834
They're great. It's so good.
-No, it's very funny.

00:06:16.834 --> 00:06:21.100
-The first song is
"The Ballad of Sweeney Todd,"

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.434
and it's just powerful
and loud and fun and great.

00:06:23.434 --> 00:06:25.200
♪ The Ballad of Sweeney... ♪
-Yeah.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.133
-People were singing.
It's unbelievable.

00:06:27.133 --> 00:06:30.067
And then you come out,
and people go...

00:06:30.067 --> 00:06:31.968
The show I was at.

00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:34.767
And then I didn't get to hear
your first line.

00:06:34.767 --> 00:06:37.267
-I know. It's so sad.
-Yeah.

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:40.667
-It's this epic opening number.

00:06:40.667 --> 00:06:42.601
The whole ensemble stands
in a line in the front,

00:06:42.601 --> 00:06:44.501
and the sopranos are just
singing these high D's,

00:06:44.501 --> 00:06:45.968
and it's just -- it's amazing.

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:47.667
And I get pulled out
of the grave, and I've got my --

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:50.634
my big first line that --
I think we just finished

00:06:50.634 --> 00:06:51.901
about 250 performances.

00:06:51.901 --> 00:06:54.067
I don't think
anybody has ever heard

00:06:54.067 --> 00:06:55.901
the first line that I have
in the show.

00:06:55.901 --> 00:06:57.701
It's a good problem to have.
People are cheering.

00:06:57.701 --> 00:06:59.334
But it's Sondheim.

00:06:59.334 --> 00:07:01.968
You're not gonna vamp music
in a Sondheim show.

00:07:01.968 --> 00:07:03.767
You're gonna sing it
exactly when it's written.

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:05.200
It's just
when people are clapping.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.234
So usually people hear me
on the second line,

00:07:07.234 --> 00:07:09.367
but not the first line.
-That's so unbelievable.

00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:12.868
I was wondering --
I mean, since you're here

00:07:12.868 --> 00:07:15.167
and we have a great audience...

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:16.667
We don't have to...
-We do.

00:07:16.667 --> 00:07:17.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:17.934 --> 00:07:19.767
♪♪

00:07:19.767 --> 00:07:21.534
-Jimmy, can I --

00:07:21.534 --> 00:07:23.601
Can I sing
the first line of Sweeney --

00:07:23.601 --> 00:07:25.033
of my entrance as Sweeney Todd?

00:07:25.033 --> 00:07:26.534
-Can you please,
for the first time ever...

00:07:26.534 --> 00:07:28.000
-For people who maybe
have been to the show

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.667
and have not heard
my first line as Sweeney Todd?

00:07:29.667 --> 00:07:31.267
-Just the first line.
That's all I want to hear.

00:07:31.267 --> 00:07:32.968
-Can I go center stage
and sing it with the Roots?

00:07:32.968 --> 00:07:34.667
-Yes, you can!
-Oh, my God!

00:07:34.667 --> 00:07:37.167
♪♪

00:07:37.167 --> 00:07:39.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:39.367 --> 00:07:40.968
[ Cheers and applause stops ]

00:07:40.968 --> 00:07:46.033
♪♪

00:07:46.033 --> 00:07:48.834
-♪ Attend the tale
of Sweeney Todd ♪

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:51.701
-There he is!
That's what I'm talking about!

00:07:51.701 --> 00:07:54.334
-Ohh! Thank you so much!

00:07:54.334 --> 00:07:56.501
-Oh! Oh!

00:07:56.501 --> 00:07:59.067
♪♪

00:07:59.067 --> 00:08:02.133
-Absolutely.
Josh Groban, everybody!

00:08:02.133 --> 00:08:04.567
Oh, my gosh, Josh.
You were amazing.

00:08:04.567 --> 00:08:06.501
-Wow. Thank you so. much.
Thank you.

00:08:06.501 --> 00:08:09.634
-Go see him in "Sweeney Todd"
on Broadway right now!

